## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-word bit ops and branchless parity reduction. It exercises combining two 64-bit halves and a compact reduction, scalable to wider architectures.

## Key Concepts

- Parity via XOR folding
- 128-bit composition hi^lo
- Branchless reductions

## Code Example

```c
int parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  return x & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to a 256-bit value?  - Can you adapt this for streaming parity on large data chunks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-byte parallel popcount without loops, scales to 128-bit data, and checks careful bit-twiddling across halves.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR per-byte popcount
- 128-bit lane handling via hi/lo halves
- No branches, fixed sequence of masks/shifts

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 countBytes128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo;
  uint64_t b = x.hi;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint128 r = { .hi = b, .lo = a };
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to count bits in arbitrary 16-byte vectors with fewer operations? 
- What are the effects of endianness on byte ordering in different compilers and architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates capability to manipulate multi-word integers with pure bit tricks, a common requirement in crypto and low-level systems programming. It tests cross-lane thinking and branch-free implementation.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit propagation between hi and lo.
- Branch-free 128-bit rotation using only shifts and OR.

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft1281(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to rotate left by k (0 <= k < 128) without branches?
- How would you test boundary cases like k=0, k=127, across hi/lo boundaries?

## Why This Is Asked
Reversing 128 bits tests a candidate's ability to decompose 128-bit data into two 64-bit lanes and apply a branch-free bit-reversal at the lower level.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value represented as {hi, lo}
- constant-time 64-bit bit-reversal using masks
- composing to achieve full 128-bit reversal without branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(v){
  v = ((v >>> 1) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((v & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  v = ((v >>> 2) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((v & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  v = ((v >>> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) | ((v & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n);
  v = ((v >>> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) | ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n);
  v = ((v >>> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) | ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n);
  v = (v >>> 32) | (v << 32);
  return v;
}
function reverseBits128(x){
  // x.hi, x.lo are 64-bit values represented as BigInt
  return { hi: reverse64(x.lo), lo: reverse64(x.hi) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for a 256-bit type if provided?
- What are the trade-offs of using 64-bit reversals vs a single 128-bit table lookup on platforms with broad SIMD support?

## Why This Is Asked

In high-throughput diffing pipelines, diffusion-boosting byte-wise bit reversal can be done with a fixed, branchless sequence, testing mastery of multi-stage bit manipulations.

## Key Concepts

- Parallel bit-twiddling across 8-byte lanes
- Masks: 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F, 0x3333333333333333, 0x5555555555555555
- Branchless, constant-time operations

## Code Example

```javascript
function reverseBitsInBytes64(n) {
  n = ((n >>> 4n) | (n << 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  n = ((n >>> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  n = ((n >>> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  return n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test edge cases (0, all ones, alternating patterns)?
- How does endianness affect results across platforms?

## Why This Is Asked
3D Morton codes enable fast spatial indexing in grid-like domains; a branchless, fixed-bit spreading approach scales predictably. 

## Key Concepts
- Morton codes for 3D require interleaving bits from three coordinates.
- Bit spreading (dilating) inserts two zero bits between each original bit; a fixed sequence of masks/shifts yields a branchless implementation.
- BigInt vs. Number in JavaScript: use BigInt for 64-bit results if implementing in JS; otherwise 64-bit types in C/C++ handle it natively.

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n) {
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn; // 21 bits
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n)) & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n)) & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n)) & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x, y, z) {
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify correctness for random inputs? 
- How to adapt to larger coordinates or alternative word sizes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of compact bit-twiddling techniques to count bits without loops, a common interview staple for performance-critical code.

## Key Concepts

- Parallel bit counting (SIMD-style within a register)
- Bit masks and shifts for aggregation
- Portability across unsigned integers and compilers

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t n) {
  n = n - ((n >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  n = (n + (n >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  return (n * 0x01010101) >> 24;
}
```

